{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q17",
  "speechText": "One example was a production issue with large PDF processing jobs. During OCR, the memory usage kept increasing, and eventually the worker crashed. That's because I did not want to assume the worker itself was the real problem. I wanted to find the root cause first. For example, I traced the processing flow, added targeted logging, checked the memory usage at important steps, and tested different batch sizes. From that, I found that the main bottleneck was the OCR CPU inference. So first, I made the current flow more stable with smaller batches and checkpoint and recovery logic, so if one step failed, we did not need to restart the whole job. Then I handled the backend and workflow side and worked with DevOps to move the heavy OCR inference to a remote GPU service. So overall, I did not only focus on stopping the crash. I also checked worker stability, memory usage, job completion, and processing time to make sure the new solution was stable, observable, and safe to roll out.",
